    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d214">

                <pb facs="0332" n="252" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg252"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Mutual Guarantee (Locarno)/465:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dodd</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-17T19:00:00+01:00">March 17, 1936—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 17—4:27 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>80. The Foreign Office has just informed us of the receipt of a communication
                    from the British Government<anchor n="5" xml:id="d214-f1"/> assuring it of the
                    British Government’s interest in a peaceful solution of the present situation
                    and stating that any proposals made in London either by <pb facs="0333" n="253"
                        xml:id="pg253"/> the German Government or others should be discussed in due
                    course. The German Government considers this the best that can be expected under
                    the circumstances and has telegraphed Avenol that the German delegation will be
                    in London Thursday morning, Ribbentrop to be the representative. He will be
                    accompanied by Dieckhoff and Woermann of the Foreign Office, the latter being of
                    the Legal Section.</p>
                <p>These men represent different elements here and Dieckhoff is very able, liberal
                    and well informed. This eases tension here a good deal.</p>
                <p>The German Government realizes that the first days will not be pleasant and seems
                    prepared to face this with the idea that the German Government will thereafter
                    have the opportunity to present its proposals.</p>
                <p>Will cable further important news when we are able to get any.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Paris, Rome, Geneva.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dodd</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
